# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore - what NEVER enters the build context
# =============================================================================
# THIS IS NOT THE SAME FILE AS .gitignore, and the difference matters.
#
# Before Docker runs a single instruction it TARS THE ENTIRE DIRECTORY and sends
# it to the daemon. Without this file that tarball includes node_modules (~400MB)
# and .git - so every build starts with a multi-second upload of files that are
# about to be thrown away or, worse, copied in.
#
# THE SECURITY HALF, which matters more than the speed half:
#   - `.env` holds real secrets. `COPY . .` in the build stage would bake them
#     into a layer. Deleting them in a LATER instruction does NOT remove them -
#     layers are immutable and anyone with the image can read them with
#     `docker history` or by extracting the layer tarball. A secret that ever
#     entered an image is leaked, permanently.
#   - `.git` contains every past commit, including secrets you already rotated
#     out of the working tree.
#
# THE CORRECTNESS HALF: excluding `node_modules` is what makes the multi-stage
# build honest. If the host's tree were copied in, a Linux image could end up
# running native modules compiled for Windows or macOS - and the failure appears
# at runtime, not at build time.
# =============================================================================

# Reinstalled inside the image by `npm ci` - and must be, for the target platform
node_modules

# Rebuilt by `npm run build`. Copying a stale host build is a classic
# "why is my fix not in the image" bug.
dist
coverage

# History, including anything ever committed and later removed
.git
.gitignore

# SECRETS. `.env.*` covers .env.local, .env.production and so on.
.env
.env.*
# The template is safe and useful documentation inside the image.
!.env.example

# Documentation - the docs/ tree here is large and changes constantly, so
# including it would invalidate the build cache on every prose edit.
*.md
!README.md

.vscode
.idea

# Test setup, fixtures and e2e specs. `tsconfig.build.json` already excludes
# them from compilation; excluding them from the CONTEXT means editing a test
# does not invalidate the Docker cache either.
test
